wang shuelien is 44  and looks forward to retiring at the age  of 50 but she may need to wait a little  longer the chinese government is set to  raise the age of retirement by at least  five years just one of the policy  changes expected in response to the  country's rapidly ageing society  i couldn't accept my retirement being  delayed much longer  i was very concerned when i heard this  news  according to the results of a once in a  decade census china's population  is greying and growing at its slowest  pace in decades  since 2010 it expanded by 5.38  to 1.41 billion people falling short of  the government target of 1.42  analysts say china is facing a  demographic time bomb  and the numbers may be lower than  reported  in the 1980s 25 million babies were born  every year last year the figure was 12  million  even if you believe this number is less  than half  of what it was the workforce is  declining quickly  and china is losing its economic  vitality the population is expected to  shrink as early  as 2022 this will impact china's ability  to compete with other countries  china's labor force will lose an  estimated 35 million workers over the  next five years  people are also living longer putting  immense pressure on china's pension and  health system  almost 20 percent of the population are  over the age of 60.  china's aging crisis is made worse by  the slowing birth rate  families are having fewer children  despite government efforts to boost the  birth rate  by ending the one child policy in 2016.  the rising cost of living is one but not  the only reason women are having fewer  children  the real problem is actually the  long-term career  there is no really social arrangement  or government support for women with  children  and how their career should be rebooted  the government is under pressure to  abandon child limits altogether  some say it's too late populations in  china's northeast are already shrinking  beijing is focusing on what it says are  positive changes  increased urbanization and higher levels  of education  it's dismissed claims its census figures  are inaccurate  wong shuiyan accepts that policy changes  are coming  but hopes workers like her will end up  better  and not worse off katrina you al jazeera  beijing
 

